from pathlib import Path
import json
p=Path('content/networks/03-tcp/tcp-connections.questions.json');q=json.loads(p.read_text())
q[0]['prompt']+=' Assume neither SYN carries payload and there is no earlier data transmission.'
q[1]['prompt']='A packet capture for a TCP connection attempt shows one case where the client receives a valid RST immediately, and another where repeated SYN attempts receive no response before the configured timeout. Which explanation is consistent with this evidence?'
q[1]['options'][0]['text']='The RST case could be an unfiltered host with no listener; the silent case could be a firewall dropping the SYNs'
q[1]['options'][0]['explanation']='Correct. These are possible explanations, not unique diagnoses: a firewall can actively reject, while loss, routing failures or an unavailable host can also cause silence.'
q[1]['options'][1]['explanation']='Silent dropping does not itself generate the observed immediate RST. A normal unfiltered TCP endpoint with no listener normally rejects a SYN.'
q[1]['options'][2]['explanation']='The capture explicitly distinguishes a received valid RST from silence. SYN-backlog overflow can trigger drops or cookies, with behaviour depending on configuration.'
q[1]['options'][3]['explanation']='The captures show distinct protocol outcomes. Neither error alone proves the service host is powered off.'
q[1]['workedExample']='1. A valid RST to the opening SYN rejects the attempt and can produce ECONNREFUSED.\n2. No response leaves retry/timeout handling to the stack and application.\n3. Neither observation alone identifies the exact device or failure cause.\n4. Do not infer a universal Linux timeout from a 1, 2, 4… series: retrieved kernel documentation includes configurable linear initial SYN timeouts as well as exponential backoff.'
q[2]['prompt']='A web server sends a FIN after its response. The browser receives and acknowledges that FIN before sending its own. Assume a successful sequential graceful close, no resets and no unread-data abort. Which endpoint enters TIME_WAIT?'
q[2]['options'][1]['explanation']='Both can enter TIME_WAIT on a simultaneous close. The question instead specifies a sequential close with the server active first.'
q[2]['options'][2]['explanation']='TIME_WAIT belongs to normal graceful closure; an accepted abort normally discards connection state without that FIN-close sequence.'
q[2]['workedExample']=q[2]['workedExample'].replace('Rule: TIME_WAIT lands on whoever sent the **first** FIN.','In this specified sequential close, the server enters TIME_WAIT. A simultaneous close can put both peers there.')
q[3]['options'][0]['explanation']='Correct. Retained state allows another ACK of a repeated FIN. MSL is not a retransmission timer, and exact reset/error outcomes after state deletion depend on processing.'
q[3]['options'][2]['explanation']='TIME_WAIT is not an application read-grace mechanism. An application can initiate a half-close with shutdown while retaining a descriptor, so it need not already have discarded every socket reference.'
q[3]['options'][3]['explanation']='The congestion window limits outstanding transmitted data; TIME_WAIT is not a wait for that numeric limit to “drain”.'
q[3]['workedExample']='1. If the peer missed the final ACK, retained state can acknowledge a retransmitted FIN; receiving the FIN can restart the TIME_WAIT timer.\n2. The specified waiting interval also protects later connection incarnations from old duplicates under the assumed maximum segment lifetime.\n3. The peer’s retransmission timeout is separate from MSL, so “one MSL until an ACK vanishes plus one for the FIN” is not a valid timer derivation.\n4. Standards and implementations define constrained reuse cases; this is not an unconditional prohibition on any earlier tuple reuse.'
q[4]['prompt']='A Python service approaches its descriptor limit. Process-level inspection confirms thousands of its sockets remain in CLOSE_WAIT long after their handlers have finished, with no intended half-close work. What should be investigated first?'
q[4]['options'][0]['explanation']='The evidence concerns descriptors retained by this process in CLOSE_WAIT. Normal TIME_WAIT state after final descriptor closure does not retain those application descriptors.'
q[4]['options'][1]['explanation']='Correct. Completed handlers retaining these sockets suggest a lifecycle/ownership leak. CLOSE_WAIT alone can also be legitimate while an application finishes sending, but the prompt excludes that case.'
q[4]['options'][2]['explanation']='A SYN flood targets establishment resources; the observed sockets have progressed to peer-FIN processing and are retained by completed handlers.'
q[4]['options'][3]['explanation']='Missing establishment ACKs do not explain these already retained CLOSE_WAIT sockets. TCP has additional simultaneous-open paths, so the full state machine is richer than one ESTABLISHED-only rule.'
q[4]['workedExample']='1. A processed peer FIN leaves the local sending direction open in CLOSE_WAIT.\n2. This prompt confirms handlers have finished but their descriptors remain open. Audit ownership and cleanup paths.\n3. For example, this deliberately incomplete handler leaves an explicitly retained connection open:\n\n```python\ndef handle(pool):\n    conn = pool.get()\n    data = conn.recv(4096)\n    if not data:\n        return  # missing ownership cleanup\n```\n\n4. Under the exercise contract, pool.get transfers cleanup responsibility while the pool retains a reference, so garbage collection does not close it. Actual pools may require return-to-pool instead of close: follow their API. Test cleanup on normal return and exceptions.'
q[6]['prompt']='On a Linux kernel built with SYN-cookie support and tcp_syncookies = 1, an overflowing SYN backlog triggers cookie mode for a new attempt. What is the main mechanism?'
q[6]['options'][0]['explanation']='Cookie mode avoids retaining the normal per-attempt half-open entry. It still uses shared secrets, processing time and network capacity.'
q[6]['options'][1]['explanation']='That is not the cookie mechanism. Source filtering may help separately, depending on reliable information and deployment.'
q[6]['options'][3]['text']='It omits the normal half-open entry, encodes reconstructable state in the sequence-number construction, and validates the returning ACK before creating the connection'
q[6]['options'][3]['explanation']='Correct. The ACK acknowledges the cookie sequence number. Validation uses secret-dependent information and freshness checks; it reduces state-exhaustion risk without eliminating CPU/bandwidth costs or every possible guess.'
q[6]['workedExample']='1. Normal stateful processing would retain a half-open attempt.\n2. Under overflow-triggered cookie mode, the server instead generates a checkable sequence number without keeping that ordinary entry.\n3. A responding client acknowledges the sequence number, modulo 2^32.\n4. The server validates the acknowledgement according to its implementation and reconstructs supported state.\n5. An off-path spoofer lacking the response has to guess; a finite authenticator is not impossible to guess, and other server resources can still be exhausted.'
q[6]['link']={'title':'RFC 4987: TCP SYN flooding and mitigations','url':'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4987'}
q[7]['prompt']='A model proxy starts with all 28,232 ports in range 32768–60999 free on one source IP. It attempts 600 short connections/s to 10.0.0.5:8080 and performs each sequential active close. Assume negligible active duration, exactly 60 seconds of TIME_WAIT tuple occupancy, no early reuse, and no other limits. What happens?'
q[7]['options'][1]['text']='The desired rate needs 36,000 occupied tuples, exceeding the 28,232 available; attempts eventually fail for lack of tuples'
q[7]['options'][1]['explanation']='Correct. Supporting 600 successful closes/s with 60 seconds of occupancy would require 36,000 tuples. They cannot all exist within this restricted port pool, so that rate cannot be sustained.'
q[7]['workedExample']='1. Four tuple fields are fixed; only source port varies.\n2. The restricted range contains 60999 − 32768 + 1 = 28,232 ports.\n3. The desired steady state would require 600 × 60 = 36,000 occupied tuples, beyond capacity.\n4. Starting empty, the model reaches capacity after about 28,232 / 600 = 47.05 seconds, before any 60-second occupancy expires.\n5. Connection reuse or more usable endpoint tuples can help. Actual Linux results depend on allocation, binds, reserved ports and safe-reuse rules; this model deliberately excludes them.'
q[8]['prompt']='For a SYN-cookie implementation that stores no ordinary per-attempt half-open entry and encodes MSS/limited option state in finite cookie space, which trade-offs can arise? Select all that apply.'
q[8]['options'][0]['text']='MSS may need to be represented by one of a limited set of supported values'
q[8]['options'][0]['explanation']='Correct for the stated design. The size and contents of that set are implementation-specific; a universal eight-value table is not assumed.'
q[8]['options'][1]['text']='Option negotiation can be restricted unless additional recoverable state is encoded, for example using timestamp fields'
q[8]['options'][1]['explanation']='Correct. Support depends on the cookie construction, kernel version and client options; finite sequence-number space constrains the information available.'
q[8]['options'][2]['text']='There is no autonomous per-attempt SYN+ACK retransmission timer without the corresponding state'
q[8]['options'][2]['explanation']='Correct. A client retransmitting its SYN can still receive another SYN+ACK; “no retransmission timer” does not mean the server can never respond again.'
q[8]['options'][4]['explanation']='Linux’s documented value 1 uses cookies on backlog overflow; value 2 enables unconditional generation when supported. Enabling the feature does not invariably mean all attempts use it.'
q[8]['workedExample']='1. Limited encoded state can constrain MSS and option support.\n2. Shared secrets remain, but this design does not retain a normal half-open entry with its own retransmission timer.\n3. Repeated client SYNs may still trigger repeated replies.\n4. Ordinary clients need no cookie-specific processing; they acknowledge the received sequence number.\n5. Linux cookie-use policy depends on its configured mode. The first three options describe genuine trade-offs.'
q[9]['prompt']='Both peers initiate graceful close. Each processes the other’s FIN before receiving any acknowledgement of its own FIN; the crossing FINs do not acknowledge those new FINs. All prior data has been processed and the later ACKs are valid. Which state sequence does each follow?'
q[10]['options'][1]['explanation']='Correct for the stated established-connection RFC 5961 rule. A rate-limited challenge ACK may be used. The mechanism improves resistance to blind resets; it is not peer authentication or proof that side channels cannot exist.'
q[10]['options'][3]['explanation']='The specified response is a challenge ACK toward the claimed peer, not a reset. It asks the peer’s TCP state to respond consistently; this is not a cryptographic proof.'
q[10]['workedExample']='1. The older in-window test accepted a range of possible sequence values. In a simplified stationary 65,536-byte window, a uniform random 32-bit guess hits that range with probability 1/65,536.\n2. RFC 5961’s established-state rule accepts an exact RCV.NXT match as a reset; an in-window nonmatch triggers a challenge ACK, subject to limiting; out-of-window resets are discarded.\n3. In an idealised stationary state with no feedback, guessing one exact 32-bit value is harder. Real state motion, rate limits, side channels and deployment details prevent interpreting this as a measured attack cost or complete authentication.'
p.write_text(json.dumps(q,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
